A||||10||BroCar 87||SIDE MOLDINGS: KEEP OR MAKE SMOOTHE???||||6||
Z||000000||BroCar 87||08-14-2000||10:20 PM||||OK Guys. I have been thinking about this for a while and I think it would look smoothe if I took off the moldings and made it flush panel to panel. The only draw back that I see is that IF I get into an accident and break a fender (God forbid) I wouldn't have a way of replacing that ONE fender. <BR>On the plus side, it would look flush and SMOOTHE. MUCH like the shaved handels and such on other vehicles. <BR>I am just throwing this idea around so... let me know what you guys think.<BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||6
Z||000001||contra||08-14-2000||10:34 PM||||i was going to do the same thing<BR>i hate moldings.<BR>even on the new corvettes, you're crazy if you get that molding streak in the scoops, it looks so much cleaner without it.<BR>but low and behold, i change my body panels alot or at least i used to but now i'm finished building up my car, so i couldn't bare to fill in the moldings flush because i wouldn't be able to get at important rivets.<BR>however i might try and fabricate a molding replacement that fits in the same slot, but fits really snug, and is level with the body panels so you wont notice it very much. it'll still be a molding, but it'll stick out less.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||08-15-2000||12:57 AM||||I think it would look great all smoothed out.  Just need to figure out a way to be able to remove the panels...<BR>Now don't laugh too hard at my novice photo editing skills... <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87gtno_molding.jpg"> <BR>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 08-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||loafer87gt||08-15-2000||01:35 AM||||I was looking at the Lotus Esprit moldings and the back moldings around the tailights are almost identical to what is on the GT fastbacks. The side moldings, however, are almost flush, but are quite a bit wider than the Fiero moldings. I was looking around at the new cavaliers and it looks like they have the same sort of moldings as the esprit, and wonder how difficult it would be to modify them to fit a fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000004||BroCar 87||08-15-2000||02:07 AM||||STANDARD... thats not a bad job! thats what i am going for. I like the smoothed look. I have the round type of moldings on my 87 coupe so its not the distaste of moldings as much as how I want mine to be more original, or custom than everyone else's.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><BR>Thanks STANDARD, I was wondering how it would look. I think i am going to go ahead and do it... i know the consiquences of not being able to take off the panels, but unless someone can convince me not to do this, I am going GO FOR IT!!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||6
Z||000005||nivlek68||08-15-2000||09:00 AM||||what about some small plugs in the channel,so you still get to the fastners. just a thought i was thinking the same thing and remaove the door handles to.||||reg||6
Z||000006||contra||08-15-2000||09:27 AM||||Brocar, if you're going to do it, keep in mind that you'll have some trouble removing part of the molding on the front fascia unless you hacksaw it off maybe. it's not removable, i think it's part of the fascia itself. Also for turn signal/running lights on the sides, i'd recommend getting some of those side blinkers like the volkswagons have, and some rice cars. they're small, easy to install, and you can get a variety of colours too. i think that would look fairly neat.||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||08-15-2000||10:57 AM||||Guys,<P>I hate to be a "wet blanket" but what your proposing may prove to be more difficult then you think. You can probably fill the doors, rear part of the fenders and the forward section of the quarters but the front and rear euro-bumpers will be a problem. Unless you find something real flexible, the seam will crack. Also, the molding extends all the way around the rear bumper. How would go about filing and molding that piece? Now, if you molded a replacement out of fiberglass, you could just about hide everything.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000008||BroCar 87||08-15-2000||01:23 PM||||MrFixit, your right. I just went out to the car and noticed the front and rear moldings of the bumpers are one piece with the bumpers.<BR>Looks like that option is out the window, unless I can find out a way to get the "fake" moldings off.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||11
Z||000009||Formula||08-15-2000||03:55 PM||||you could just paint them like how new cars are||||reg||1
Z||000010||trigger||08-16-2000||07:01 PM||||What if you made a flat piece of fiberglass to fill in the trim and make fasteners for it?  Or you could take the panels off, smooth over the mold opening, and use fasteners on the back of the fender similar to the tree fastener for trim and door panels.  That way if you need to get a panel off break them like trim clips and buy news ones to put on?  Would you use remote doors.  If you battery dies or especially if your doors freeze shut you could have a problem.  Hope this helps.  <P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
